cells:
- kind: 1
  value: |
    # Generate embeddings inside Oracle AI Database

    This optional path shows what changes when embedding generation itself is brought into the database. A compatible ONNX embedding model is prepared and imported during environment setup, after which SQL and PL/SQL can invoke that database-resident model close to the application data.

    **We will:**
    1. Confirm that the prepared embedding model is available.
    2. Inspect the registered model and its input/output metadata.
    3. Generate embeddings with `VECTOR_EMBEDDING`.
    4. Generate embeddings from PL/SQL with `DBMS_VECTOR.UTL_TO_EMBEDDING`.
    5. Store embeddings with application data.
    6. Generate a query embedding and use it for semantic search.
  languageId: markdown

- kind: 1
  value: |
    ## 1. Confirm the prepared model

    **Why this matters:**

    Model import is environment preparation. Keeping it outside the main walkthrough means the notebook can focus on the database-development change: generating and using embeddings from the same database that owns the application data.

    **What changes for development:**
    - the embedding model becomes a database-managed dependency;
    - application SQL and PL/SQL can invoke that model without a separate embedding endpoint for this path;
    - model deployment can be prepared once and then reused by the development workflow.
  languageId: markdown

- kind: 2
  value: |
    SELECT model_name,
           mining_function,
           algorithm,
           algorithm_type,
           model_size
    FROM user_mining_models
    WHERE model_name = 'DEMO_MINILM';
  languageId: oracle-sql

- kind: 1
  value: |
    ## 2. Inspect the registered model

    **Why this matters:**

    Embeddings are only useful when the model's input and output contract is understood. Checking the registered model before building an application pipeline helps the developer confirm that the expected vector representation is available in the database.

    **What changes for development:**
    - model metadata becomes queryable from the database;
    - the developer can validate the model definition before using it in application SQL or PL/SQL.
  languageId: markdown

- kind: 2
  value: |
    SELECT model_name,
           attribute_name,
           attribute_type,
           data_type,
           vector_info
    FROM user_mining_model_attributes
    WHERE model_name = 'DEMO_MINILM'
    ORDER BY attribute_name;
  languageId: oracle-sql

- kind: 1
  value: |
    ## 3. Generate an embedding with `VECTOR_EMBEDDING`

    **Why this matters:**

    When an application needs semantic representations, a developer otherwise has to call an embedding model somewhere outside the database and move the result back to the data tier. A database-resident model makes embedding creation available as a SQL expression.

    **What changes for development:**
    - generate embeddings directly in SQL;
    - compose embedding generation with inserts, updates, transformations, and searches;
    - keep the text and its semantic representation in the same data-processing workflow.
  languageId: markdown

- kind: 2
  value: |
    SELECT VECTOR_EMBEDDING(
             DEMO_MINILM USING
             'Oracle Database can generate embeddings close to the data.' AS DATA
           ) AS embedding
    FROM dual;
  languageId: oracle-sql

- kind: 1
  value: |
    The result is a database `VECTOR` generated by the registered model.
  languageId: markdown

- kind: 1
  value: |
    ## 4. Generate an embedding from PL/SQL

    **Why this matters:**

    Database applications often have PL/SQL ingestion, transformation, or batch-processing logic. Those workflows need a procedural API when embedding generation is one step in a larger database operation.

    **What changes for development:**
    - call embedding generation from PL/SQL-oriented workflows;
    - avoid moving text out of the database solely to obtain a vector;
    - use the same database-resident model from both SQL and procedural database code.
  languageId: markdown

- kind: 2
  value: |
    SELECT DBMS_VECTOR.UTL_TO_EMBEDDING(
             'Oracle Database can generate embeddings inside the database.',
             JSON('{"provider":"database","model":"DEMO_MINILM"}')
           ) AS embedding
    FROM dual;
  languageId: oracle-sql

- kind: 1
  value: |
    ## 5. Store embeddings with application data

    **Why this matters:**

    An embedding becomes useful to the application when it stays associated with the source record. Keeping source text and its semantic representation together simplifies loading, lifecycle management, and retrieval.

    **What changes for development:**
    - generate and persist vectors as part of normal data loading;
    - keep source content and its semantic representation in the same database workflow;
    - make the resulting data immediately available to SQL-based semantic search.
  languageId: markdown

- kind: 2
  value: |
    CREATE TABLE ai_embedding_docs (
      doc_id    NUMBER GENERATED BY DEFAULT AS IDENTITY,
      title     VARCHAR2(200),
      content   VARCHAR2(4000),
      embedding VECTOR
    );

    INSERT INTO ai_embedding_docs (title, content, embedding)
    VALUES (
      'Database-native embeddings',
      'Oracle AI Database can generate embeddings close to application data.',
      VECTOR_EMBEDDING(
        DEMO_MINILM USING
        'Oracle AI Database can generate embeddings close to application data.' AS DATA
      )
    );

    INSERT INTO ai_embedding_docs (title, content, embedding)
    VALUES (
      'Semantic retrieval',
      'Vector similarity can retrieve content by meaning rather than exact keywords.',
      VECTOR_EMBEDDING(
        DEMO_MINILM USING
        'Vector similarity can retrieve content by meaning rather than exact keywords.' AS DATA
      )
    );

    COMMIT;
  languageId: oracle-sql

- kind: 2
  value: |
    SELECT doc_id,
           title,
           VECTOR_DIMENSION_COUNT(embedding) AS dimensions
    FROM ai_embedding_docs
    ORDER BY doc_id;
  languageId: oracle-sql

- kind: 1
  value: |
    ## 6. Generate the query embedding and search by meaning

    **Why this matters:**

    Stored embeddings and query embeddings need to occupy the same semantic space. Using the same database-resident model for both sides keeps that model choice explicit and consistent.

    **What changes for development:**
    - create the query vector inside the SQL statement;
    - compare it directly with stored vectors;
    - build an end-to-end database-native semantic-search path when that architecture fits the application.
  languageId: markdown

- kind: 2
  value: |
    SELECT title,
           content,
           VECTOR_DISTANCE(
             embedding,
             VECTOR_EMBEDDING(
               DEMO_MINILM USING 'finding similar content by meaning' AS DATA
             ),
             COSINE
           ) AS cosine_distance
    FROM ai_embedding_docs
    ORDER BY cosine_distance
    FETCH FIRST 5 ROWS ONLY;
  languageId: oracle-sql

- kind: 1
  value: |
    ## What changes for the database developer?

    A database-resident embedding model changes the application boundary: model inference can become part of the same SQL/PLSQL workflow that owns the data.

    **Model object** -> a managed database dependency.

    **SQL / PL/SQL embedding APIs** -> generate vectors close to the data.

    **VECTOR storage** -> persist the semantic representation with application rows.

    **Similarity search** -> use the same model for stored and query embeddings.

    This can remove an external embedding-service dependency for supported models and environments. It does not remove the need to choose, obtain, validate, maintain, and govern the embedding model.
  languageId: markdown

- kind: 1
  value: |
    ## References

    Oracle documentation:
    - https://docs.oracle.com/en/database/oracle/oracle-database/26/vecse/import-onnx-models-oracle-ai-database-end-end-example.html
    - https://docs.oracle.com/en/database/oracle/oracle-database/26/vecse/sql-quick-start-using-vector-embedding-model-uploaded-database.html
    - https://docs.oracle.com/en/database/oracle/oracle-database/26/vecse/convert-text-string-embedding-oracle-ai-database.html
    - https://docs.oracle.com/en/database/oracle/oracle-database/26/vecse/dbms_vector-vecse.html

    Autonomous AI Database:
    - https://docs.oracle.com/en/cloud/paas/autonomous-database/serverless/adbsb/select-ai-examples.html
    - https://docs.oracle.com/en/cloud/paas/autonomous-database/serverless/adbsb/autonomous-always-free.html

    Oracle practitioner material:
    - https://blogs.oracle.com/developers/how-to-load-embedding-models-into-oracle-ai-database-in-2026
    - https://blogs.oracle.com/developers/building-onnx-embedding-workflows-in-oracle-ai-database-with-python
  languageId: markdown
